{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["calibration","discrimination","AUC","F1-score","tree ensembles","tabular classification"],"description":"The evidence primarily focuses on calibration improvements in Brier score, ECE, and log loss, but does not systematically evaluate the trade-off between calibration and discrimination (e.g., AUC, precision-recall, or F1-score) for tree ensembles on tabular classification. While E1 and E2 show improved calibration, they do not report or analyze changes in discrimination metrics after applying isotonic regression, leaving a critical gap in understanding the cost of calibration in terms of model discriminative power.","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"id":"G1","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"6935c46dc7c0aedcc4676d1f4c6e0ac1c2a7038e606c9cbd14e5b4571166ff59","kind":"artefact","prev":"63a91ac53b61abcbb6ca109771b20168c1c6c7d56adb97b050ece3d933080fb6","seq":16,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.727+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["tree ensembles","XGBoost","LightGBM","CatBoost","tabular classification"],"description":"The studies cited (E1 and E2) evaluate calibration on specific models—Random Forest and SVM—without testing a broader range of tree ensemble methods such as XGBoost, LightGBM, or CatBoost. This limits the generalizability of the findings to the wider class of tree ensembles commonly used in tabular data settings.","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"id":"G2","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"b8780c002e81a7d6bf4d6c1cb6a3ac5977b7978b16d3ca11523350a663324c0c","kind":"artefact","prev":"6935c46dc7c0aedcc4676d1f4c6e0ac1c2a7038e606c9cbd14e5b4571166ff59","seq":17,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.727+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["tabular classification","dataset characteristics","structured data"],"description":"The datasets used in the cited studies (e.g., heart disease prediction, default modeling) are not explicitly described, and there is no information on whether they are tabular or contain structured, real-world features. Thus, the generalizability of findings to typical tabular classification tasks remains unverified.","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"id":"G3","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"6e2bf6d350670a93df45af86909637530366449eff5086a0c8d911b1809f8a8a","kind":"artefact","prev":"b8780c002e81a7d6bf4d6c1cb6a3ac5977b7978b16d3ca11523350a663324c0c","seq":18,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.727+00:00"}
{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"hypothesis"},"hash":"cdba158135324a8694dfd67a54e7f6d957728493110b6e5b69d7853ac7a4cb9f","kind":"stage","prev":"6e2bf6d350670a93df45af86909637530366449eff5086a0c8d911b1809f8a8a","seq":19,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.728+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"Expected Calibration Error (ECE)","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"expected_direction":"decrease","gap_ids":["G1"],"id":"H1","independent_variable":"application of isotonic regression to tree ensemble models (e.g., Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM)","rationale":"The gap G1 highlights that existing evidence focuses on calibration improvements (e.g., ECE, Brier) without evaluating the trade-off with discrimination metrics like AUC or F1. This hypothesis directly addresses that gap by proposing a measurable trade-off between calibration and discrimination, which can be tested on public tabular datasets.","statement":"Post-hoc isotonic regression improves calibration (as measured by ECE) in tree ensembles on tabular classification tasks, but this improvement is accompanied by a statistically significant decrease in discrimination (as measured by AUC or F1-score) compared to the original ensemble model."},"hash":"4b19009fd8c824b596300f5bb542c7f762a9993dd620dcd795491aed89a853f6","kind":"artefact","prev":"cdba158135324a8694dfd67a54e7f6d957728493110b6e5b69d7853ac7a4cb9f","seq":20,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.728+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"AUC or F1-score","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"expected_direction":"decrease","gap_ids":["G1","G2"],"id":"H2","independent_variable":"application of isotonic regression to tree ensemble models","rationale":"This hypothesis expands on G1 by explicitly testing discrimination (AUC/F1) and incorporates G2 by specifying a broader class of tree ensembles (beyond Random Forest). It allows for testing on real-world tabular datasets with varying feature structures and imbalances.","statement":"Isotonic regression applied to tree ensembles (e.g., XGBoost, LightGBM) results in a measurable reduction in AUC or F1-score compared to the uncalibrated model, with the magnitude of reduction varying by dataset complexity and class imbalance."},"hash":"8d60f577613b461cc25b59f69a7242346ae6040ccab7c0cd87acc8c24bdb58a4","kind":"artefact","prev":"4b19009fd8c824b596300f5bb542c7f762a9993dd620dcd795491aed89a853f6","seq":21,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.728+00:00"}
