{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"experiment"},"hash":"27e3db413f48182c26d36f88c2a86557d2e142bb4a8a2973314ecf9878242aac","kind":"stage","prev":"52a2fb27d9852520a9c14121cedb1c2a5d42139c2ebde0d03366f68871ee900c","seq":23,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.728+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"random_forest","datasets":["breast_cancer","wine_binary","digits_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H1","id":"D1","metrics":["ece","f1","roc_auc"],"model":"random_forest","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on the trade-off between calibration (ECE) and discrimination (F1, AUC). We select three public, balanced tabular datasets (breast_cancer, wine_binary, digits_binary) to ensure diversity in feature structure and class distribution. The control condition uses the original random forest model to establish baseline performance. The treatment applies isotonic regression to the ensemble output (random_forest+isotonic) to test if ECE decreases as hypothesized. We measure both ECE (dependent variable) and F1/AUC (to assess discrimination loss) across the same datasets. A paired Wilcoxon test is used to statistically evaluate if ECE decreases significantly, while also checking for a significant drop in F1 or AUC. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness, and three seeds ensure reproducibility and reduce variance. We exclude imbalanced datasets (e.g., breast_cancer_imb10) because the hypothesis does not specify imbalance handling, and we focus on the core trade-off in standard settings.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["random_forest+isotonic"]},"hash":"5eb6d442ed222eaa0a55eb44201a729f7d2896e5ea4a98ef0c2b9853193a136d","kind":"artefact","prev":"27e3db413f48182c26d36f88c2a86557d2e142bb4a8a2973314ecf9878242aac","seq":24,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.728+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"gradient_boosting","datasets":["breast_cancer","breast_cancer_imb10","digits_binary","digits_binary_imb10","wine_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H2","id":"D2","metrics":["roc_auc","f1"],"model":"gradient_boosting","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on the effect of isotonic regression on the performance of tree ensemble models (specifically gradient boosting) in terms of AUC and F1-score. To test this, we use a controlled experiment across datasets with varying complexity and class imbalance. The control condition uses gradient boosting without any calibration (baseline), while the treatment applies isotonic regression to the gradient boosting model. We evaluate both ROC AUC and F1-score—key discriminative metrics specified in the hypothesis. Datasets are chosen to cover both balanced (e.g., breast_cancer, wine_binary) and imbalanced (e.g., breast_cancer_imb10, digits_binary_imb10) scenarios to reflect real-world variation. A paired Wilcoxon test is used because it assesses differences between related samples (calibrated vs. uncalibrated), and is appropriate for non-normal distributions in performance metrics. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness, and three random seeds ensure reproducibility and reduce variance due to randomness. The inclusion of both balanced and imbalanced datasets allows for a comprehensive evaluation of how class imbalance affects the impact of isotonic calibration, aligning with the hypothesis’s emphasis on dataset complexity and class imbalance.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["gradient_boosting+isotonic"]},"hash":"d8d61e0b8c1b9c0143474a166f21367a102ba6f311d51d4c0bf3772df7b7b108","kind":"artefact","prev":"5eb6d442ed222eaa0a55eb44201a729f7d2896e5ea4a98ef0c2b9853193a136d","seq":25,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:12:05.729+00:00"}
